course: dictionary
lesson: 14324
title: Obscure: hidden, hard, or not well known
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Obscure has more than one meaning, so the words around it tell you which one to use. Look at the clues first, then test your meaning in the sentence.
explain: «The little village is so obscure that most maps do not show it.» Obscure means not well known here. Test it: "so unknown that most maps do not show it" still makes sense.
explain: «Thick fog began to obscure the harbour.» Here obscure is a verb meaning to hide something or make it hard to see. The fog is the clue: fog hides things. Swap in "hide" and the sentence still works.
explain: «The rules were so obscure that nobody could follow them.» This time obscure means hard to understand, not clear. The clue is that nobody could follow the rules.
fact: Obscure can be an adjective, as in an obscure singer, or a verb, as in clouds obscure the moon.
fact: As an adjective, «obscure» means not well known, or hard to understand and not clear.
fact: As a verb, «obscure» means to hide something or make it hard to see or understand.
fact: «Obscured» is the past tense of the verb, as in «Clouds obscured the moon last night.»
fact: On the road, do not let anything obscure your view when you cross, so you can always see the traffic.
exercise: «The little village is so obscure that most maps do not show it.» What does obscure mean here? | Brightly lit at night | *Not well known | Very loud and noisy | Hard to reach on foot
exercise: «Thick fog began to obscure the view of the harbour.» What does obscure mean in this sentence? | Make bright and shiny | Clean and wash | *Make hard to see | Freeze solid
exercise: «The rules were so obscure that the class could not follow them.» Find and copy the word that means hard to understand. | class | *obscure | could | follow
test: «Clouds obscured the moon last night.» What does obscured mean? | Made louder | Melted away | Lit up | *Hid from view
test: Mia says, «That fact is so obscure, nobody knows it.» What does obscure mean here? | *Not well known | Very funny | Bright and clear | Loud and fast
exam: When you cross the road, why must nothing obscure your view? | So you can hear the radio better | So you can walk more slowly | So the road looks darker | *So you can see the traffic coming
